17592186065004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065003 #edb/ref 1061 1341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065003 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "88a6a7a2-ceb7-5c77-9490-120d96325eb0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065003 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065004 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065004 #edb/ref 1061 1343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065004 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f0993d66-6693-5a4b-b2b7-aea4337779d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065004 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065005 #edb/ref 1061 1345 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065005 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "82e795fa-0845-54a7-af6d-ea30b52689f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065005 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065406 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065154 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065154 #edb/ref 1061 22577 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065154 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "13ecc906-0901-5139-9a2c-d3436202e3b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065154 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065372 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065372 #edb/ref 1064 "To compute the integral of any power function $x^n,$ where $n \\neq -1,$ we can use the **power rule for integration**. The power rule for integration states that\n\n$$\n\\int x^n\\, \\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac {x^{n + 1}}{n + 1} + C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is any constant and $n\\neq -1.$ For example, in the case of $n=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{3+1}}{3+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the **antiderivative** of $f'(x) = x^3$ is $f(x) = \\dfrac {x^{4}}{4}+C .$ \n\nWe can check that this is the correct answer by taking the derivative of $f(x)$ and seeing that it is indeed equal to $x^3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{x}^{4})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4{x}^{4-1} & = \\\\ {x}^{3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the constant $C$ disappeared when we differentiated because the derivative of any constant is zero. We always add $+C$ whenever we find the antiderivative of a function because we know that it will disappear when we differentiate.\n\n**Note:** The power rule for integration is just the power rule for differentiation in reverse!\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} \\left( x^{n+1} \\right) = (n+1)x^n \\quad \\Leftrightarrow \\quad \\displaystyle \\int x^n \\, \\textrm dx = \\dfrac{x^{n+1}}{n+1}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065372 #edb/ref 1065 3852 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065372 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9f08a264-7a79-50c1-afb0-903bb2a256f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065372 #edb/ref 1067 "The Power Rule for Integration" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065405 #edb/ref 1064 "An **antiderivative** is the *opposite* of a derivative. For example:\n\n- Using the power rule, we know that the *derivative* of $\\color{blue}x^2$ is ${\\color{red}2x}.$\n\n- Going in the *opposite* direction, we say that an **antiderivative** of $\\color{red}2x$ is ${\\color{blue}x^2}.$\n\nFor any given function, there are infinitely many antiderivatives. For example:\n\n- The derivative of $\\color{blue}x^2$ is ${\\color{red}2x},$ so an *antiderivative* of $\\color{red}2x$ is ${\\color{blue}x^2}.$\n\n- The derivative of $\\color{blue}x^2+1$ is ${\\color{red}2x},$ so another *antiderivative* of $\\color{red}2x$ is ${\\color{blue}x^2+1}.$\n\n- The derivative of $\\color{blue}x^2+2$ is ${\\color{red}2x},$ so another *antiderivative* of $\\color{red}2x$ is ${\\color{blue}x^2+2}.$\n\n- ...\n\nIn general, all the antiderivatives of $\\color{red}2x$ can be written in the form ${\\color{blue}x^2+C},$ where $C$ is a constant.\n\nTo represent the antiderivative symbolically, we use a special symbol $\\displaystyle \\int$ called the **integral** symbol. So, using **integral notation**, we can write\n\n$$\n\\int 2x \\, \\textrm d x = x^2 + C\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ represents an arbitrary constant and is called the **constant of integration**.\n\nIn words, $\\displaystyle \\int 2x \\, \\textrm d x$ is said as “the integral of $2x$ with respect to $x$” or “the antiderivative of $2x$.” Integrals like this are called **indefinite integrals**, and the function being **integrated** ($2x$ in this case) is called the **integrand**." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065405 #edb/ref 1065 691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065405 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a31b3485-463c-59da-8118-836cf7949efc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065405 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065406 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's recap the main points:\n\n- Integrals like $\\displaystyle \\int x^3\\,\\textrm d x$ are called **indefinite integrals**.\n\n- Whenever we calculate an indefinite integral, we must always remember to add an **arbitrary constant** $+C.$\n\n- To integrate power functions, we can use the **power rule for integration**, which states that\n\n$$\n\\int x^n\\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac{x^{n+1}}{n+1} + C,\n$$\n\nprovided that $n \\neq -1.$ To emphasize, the power rule does *not* work when $n=-1.$ So we cannot use the above formula to work out $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{x}\\,\\textrm d x.$ Integrals like this need to be handled differently, and you'll find out how soon." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065406 #edb/ref 1065 697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065406 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a0806909-50b1-5a35-9ff6-fa9e18aa5c23" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065406 #edb/ref 1067 "Some Final Notes on Indefinite Integrals and Constants" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233174 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186514278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233174 #edb/ref 1069 "q-113665" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233174 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233174 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate $\\int {x}^{20}\\,\\text{d}x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233174 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6e6c66f6-6133-59f6-a79d-0cbf1615accf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233174 #edb/ref 1074 "We apply the power rule with $n=20$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{20}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{20+1}}{20+1}+C=\\frac{{x}^{21}}{21}+C.\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{21}}{21}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{21}}{21})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{21}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{21})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{21}\\cdot 21{x}^{20} & = \\\\ {x}^{20}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234299 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186514344 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234299 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71100" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234299 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234299 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x)={x}^{-1/7}$ and that ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234299 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "44529618-fcf7-5581-8bd3-a0df68de242f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234299 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{1}{7}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-1/7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/7+1}}{(-\\frac{1}{7}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6/7}}{(\\frac{6}{7})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{6}{x}^{6/7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502863 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502930 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502863 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502863 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate the integral of $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[7]{{x}^{13}}}$. Assume $x>0$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502863 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "054c92af-f6c0-5cb6-8c52-c81bf5740a21" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502863 #edb/ref 1074 "Note that $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[7]{{x}^{13}}}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{13/7}}={x}^{-13/7}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{13}{7}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[7]{{x}^{13}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{-13/7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-13/7+1}}{-\\frac{13}{7}+1}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{6}{x}^{-6/7}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{6{x}^{6/7}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502872 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502875 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502872 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502872 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x)={x}^{-7/9}$ and that ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$ Assume $x>0$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502872 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1ea9e5d9-602f-5599-818f-aa6b87eda7f7" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502872 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{7}{9}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-7/9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-7/9+1}}{(-\\frac{7}{9}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2/9}}{(\\frac{2}{9})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{2/9}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502876 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502924 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502876 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502876 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate the integral of $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{5}}}$. Assume $x>0$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502876 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "24409f16-92eb-5e3a-9a94-2612754d8d1a" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502876 #edb/ref 1074 "Note that $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{5}}}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{5/3}}={x}^{-5/3}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{5}{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{-5/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-5/3+1}}{-\\frac{5}{3}+1}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-2/3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2{x}^{2/3}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502879 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502903 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502879 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502879 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x)={x}^{-4/7}$ and that ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$ Assume $x>0$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502879 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2838d5a7-98fc-5b74-838a-12c9f0c1d487" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502879 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{4}{7}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-4/7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-4/7+1}}{(-\\frac{4}{7}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/7}}{(\\frac{3}{7})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}{x}^{3/7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502887 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502928 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502887 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502887 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate the integral of $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{10}}}$. Assume $x>0$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502887 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "35340db4-7b99-5255-81e2-d140281bf3c1" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502887 #edb/ref 1074 "Note that $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{10}}}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{10/3}}={x}^{-10/3}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{10}{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{10}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{-10/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-10/3+1}}{-\\frac{10}{3}+1}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{7}{x}^{-7/3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{7{x}^{7/3}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502891 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502865 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502891 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502891 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x)={x}^{-1/4}$ and that ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$ Assume $x>0$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502891 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3a5e9787-8ef9-5e19-99e9-70e48a27adcf" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502891 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{1}{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-1/4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/4+1}}{(-\\frac{1}{4}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/4}}{(\\frac{3}{4})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{3/4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502900 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502963 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502900 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502900 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{5/4}}$ and that ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$ Assume $x>0$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502900 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "40665566-8355-550d-acf0-8fd3e706266d" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502900 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{1}{{x}^{5/4}}={x}^{-5/4}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{5}{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-5/4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-5/4+1}}{(-\\frac{5}{4}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/4}}{(-\\frac{1}{4})}+C \\\\  & =-4{x}^{-1/4}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{{x}^{1/4}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502914 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502926 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502914 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502914 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x)={x}^{-2/7}$ and that ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$ Assume $x>0$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502914 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6ac48824-980d-5dff-8c73-e9a877cd9d8e" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502914 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{2}{7}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-2/7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-2/7+1}}{(-\\frac{2}{7}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5/7}}{(\\frac{5}{7})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{5}{x}^{5/7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502920 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502859 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502920 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186502920 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x)={x}^{-1/5}$ and that ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x)$, cal